Chapter: DeepNestRt

 The input shape the corpus lacked, and the emitter answered it WRONG
 rather than refusing. emit-wat-expr-at bailed at depth 256 with
 `(i64.const 0)`, so this chapter printed 44 where x86-64 printed 300,
 assembling and running clean with no diagnostic on either side.

 A `let` chain is what makes the depth reachable. Nested arithmetic
 cannot get there: it nests the emitted output too, and wat2wasm's own
 parser faults between 200 and 250 folded expressions, so the module
 never assembles and the truncation stays invisible. A `let` recurses
 without nesting what it emits, so the WAT stays flat and every tool
 downstream accepts the wrong answer.

 300 is past the old bail and inside what the front end accepts: the
 parser's own 4096-call fuel refuses this shape by 1300 and passes it at
 1000, so the emitter's 4096 backstop sits above anything that reaches
 it and cannot be graded from source.

